            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"grouping-diagnosis\">Grouping diagnosis</h2>\n<h3 id=\"healthcare-resource-groups-hrgs-why\">Healthcare Resource Groups (HRGs) - Why?</h3></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "lEQa",
      "code_hash": "ca0f2a57ef739113f20330c15015804d",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h3 id=\"what-are-healthcare-resource-groups-hrgs\">What are Healthcare Resource Groups (HRGs)?</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Healthcare Resource Groups (HRGs) are designed to be standard groupings of clinically <strong>similar treatments</strong> which use <strong>common levels of healthcare resource.</strong></li>\n<li>Crucially, they are constructed to ensure <strong>clinical meaningfulness</strong> while accurately reflecting <strong>expected resource consumption</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>How are they constructed?</strong>\nA strict algorithm (the NHS Local Grouper) categorizes patients based on:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diagnoses &amp; Procedures</strong> (ICD-10 and OPCS-4 codes)</li>\n<li><strong>Patient &amp; Care Context</strong> (Age, gender, and admission/discharge methods)</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>Why use HRGs over other grouping methods?</strong>\nWe initially evaluated standard clinical groupers like the <strong>Elixhauser Comorbidity Index</strong>. However, Elixhauser is designed for specific comorbidities and failed to categorize almost 50% of the diagnoses in our dataset. Because the NHS designed HRGs to account for total hospital activity, using HRGs guarantees that <em>every</em> admission is mapped to a clinically valid category that reflects the true intensity of care.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "PKri",
      "code_hash": "763208ced77dc3777e3133caae7df969",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h3 id=\"why-use-hrgs-for-icd-10-dimensionality-reduction\">Why Use HRGs for ICD-10 Dimensionality Reduction?</h3>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">In this study, we are estimating the causal impact of different diagnoses on <strong>Length of Stay (LoS)</strong>. Feeding raw diagnosis codes directly into our machine learning models (e.g., LightGBM) presents significant methodological challenges that HRGs perfectly solve:</span>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Solving Extreme Sparsity:</strong> We have over 800 distinct ICD10 codes in our dataset. Modeling these directly results in an excessively sparse matrix, which destabilizes causal inference and inflates variance. Grouping them by their root HRG chapter collapses these 800+ codes into ~36 highly robust categories.</li>\n<li><strong>Direct Alignment with the Target Variable:</strong> Because HRGs were explicitly engineered by the NHS to measure <em>healthcare resource consumption</em>, they inherently correlate with Length of Stay (our primary resource metric). This makes them a highly effective latent representation of the diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Reproducibility &amp; Interpretability:</strong> Rather than using an opaque mathematical clustering technique (like PCA or autoencoders) to reduce dimensions, the HRG algorithm provides a transparent, clinically validated standard. The resulting effect sizes are directly interpretable by hospital capacity planners and stakeholders.</li>\n</ol></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "Xref",
      "code_hash": "95718d94ee4f52be7af2e4136416a74b",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h3 id=\"the-anatomy-of-an-hrg-code\">The Anatomy of an HRG Code</h3>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Before detailing the grouping strategy, it is helpful to understand how an HRG code is structured. The characters in an HRG represent different levels of clinical granularity:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>2-Character HRGs (The Chapter/Sub-chapter):</strong> This is the broader clinical umbrella. The first letter denotes the main body system or specialty (e.g., \"F\" for Digestive System), and the second letter denotes the sub-category. It provides a high-level grouping.</li>\n<li><strong>4-Character HRGs (The Base HRG):</strong> This adds two numbers to the sub-chapter to identify a highly specific diagnosis or procedure group (e.g., \"FD10\" for benign colorectal neoplasms). It provides granular, highly specific clinical detail.</li>\n</ul></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "SFPL",
      "code_hash": "92b108a5bcb507b45c3929b4b8c307d8",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h3 id=\"our-methodology-frequency-based-icd-10-to-hrg-mapping\">Our Methodology: Frequency-Based ICD-10 to HRG Mapping</h3>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">To balance statistical power with clinical precision in our causal models, we did not apply a one-size-fits-all grouping. Instead, we grouped the 800+ raw ICD-10 codes based on their unique admission volumes:</span>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The Top 99% of Admissions (High Volume):</strong>\n    We mapped the most frequent ICD-10 codes to their highly specific <strong>4-character HRG groups</strong>. Because these conditions appear frequently in the dataset, our model has plenty of statistical power to handle them at a granular level.</li>\n<li><strong>The Remaining 1% of Admissions (The Long Tail):</strong>\n    For rare, very low-volume ICD-10 codes, mapping them at the 4-character level would re-introduce the exact data sparsity problem we are trying to avoid. Instead, we rolled these rare codes up to their broader <strong>2-character HRG chapters</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>Why do this?</strong>\nThis hybrid approach guarantees that <strong>100% of the ICD-10 codes are captured and utilized</strong> in the causal model. It preserves granular insights where we have the data to support it (the 99%), while safely capturing the long tail of rare diseases (the 1%) without destabilizing the machine learning algorithm.</span>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"the-final-result-36-distinct-clinical-groups\">The Final Result: 36 Distinct Clinical Groups</h3>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">After applying this frequency-based mapping strategy, we successfully collapsed the 800+ raw ICD-10 codes into just <strong>36 robust categories</strong>.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>The \"Unknown\" Cohort</strong>\nCrucially, this final set of 36 includes an <strong>\"unknown\" group</strong>. This category represents admissions where patients were not assigned a valid diagnosis code during their stay (often due to diagnostic ambiguity, pending test results, or coding delays).</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">It is important to highlight this group because it is substantial\u2014accounting for approximately <strong>28% of all admissions</strong> in our dataset. Rather than dropping these records (which would introduce severe selection bias), retaining them as a distinct category allows our model to estimate the causal impact of <em>diagnostic uncertainty itself</em> on Length of Stay.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
